<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulric Manfred II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 992–1034</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Olderico Manfredi II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulric Manfred II was margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> from about 1000 until his death in 1034, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arduinici">Arduinici</a> family. He controlled the Alpine routes over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-cenis">Mont Cenis</a> and Mont Genèvre passes, extended the march across much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and founded monasteries, including San Giusto at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-susa">Adelaide of Susa</a> inherited his lands and, through her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-count-of-savoy">Otto of Savoy</a>, brought them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>.</p></div>
